00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.840
So let's look at this
process in more details.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.860
How do you actually pick the
right VM shape on Google Cloud?

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.420
How do you match
your exact needs

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.120
to that available
from our platform?

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.340
We believe that for
90% of your workloads

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.130
this should be a very
simple process where

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.990
you need to do no
comparisons and you

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.180
need to find what you're looking
for with exactly one click.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.320
And this is why
we've really focused

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.050
on having that as one
of our core pillars

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.110
as we created our
general-purpose VM.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.100
We wanted to make them
simply truly general purpose.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.170
They are designed to
be simple, flexible,

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.950
and fit the majority
of your workload,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.740
and we do that in many ways.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.820
First of all, we focus with
our general-purpose VMs

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.550
on giving you flexible sizing.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.520
You can simply choose
the number of cores

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.700
and the amount of memory that
you need at any point of time

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.460
and with the ratio that
you're looking for.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.570
You don't have to choose from
only predefined shapes which

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.100
might map to some
workloads we have

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.682
in mind that might
not necessarily

00:04:48.682 --> 00:04:50.140
be exactly what
you're looking for.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.690
With the general-purpose
machine types,

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.210
you can choose more
memory, more cores,

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.460
or you can choose a balance.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.620
And what's very
important in this process

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.840
is that you don't
need to think or worry

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.150
about price changing
in strange ways

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.730
as you change those ratios.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:14.090
So by having a very
simple and linear pricing

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.590
for both cores and memory,
that becomes one less decision

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.210
you need to think
about and one less

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.470
factor you need to consider.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.340
It's one simple pricing that
you can have across the board.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.420
What's also very important about
the general-purpose machine

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:35.230
types is that with
the custom machine

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.950
types available on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.360
right sizing becomes something
that you can actually

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.180
do on the cloud so that
you don't overprovision

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.770
and you get exactly
what you're looking for.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.430
Let's talk about
custom machine types.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.390
As you can see
here, these sliders

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.720
are actually part of what I
like most about Google Compute

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.930
Engine, simple sliders that
allow you to just configure

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.150
the machine type to
what you have in mind

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:06.810
and what exactly fits
what you're looking at.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.640
With custom machine types,
you can build an exact fit,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.430
and you can prevent
any wasted resources.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.750
And we've seen our
customers use this feature

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:21.620
to actually benefit from more
than 19% savings versus just

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.950
using predefined instances
that are available, including

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.510
predefined instances available
on Google Compute Engine

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.010
itself.

00:06:32.838 --> 00:06:34.630
Another great way that
custom machine types

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:37.360
help you is that it provides
you with something that we

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.360
call extended memory,
and extended memory

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.640
is really meant to
break any barriers

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.010
you have to creating the
exact shape you're looking at.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.740
So for example,
let's take an example

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.960
of a workload that
has very expensive

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.820
per-core requirements.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:55.540
On any cloud, you typically
have to choose the memory

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.560
that you're looking
for, and you get

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.165
a predefined amount of
cores available with that,

00:07:00.165 --> 00:07:02.290
and then you will have to
pay the licensing for all

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.040
your cores.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.080
You don't have any choice.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.120
With extended memory,
you can actually

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.550
figure out the amount
of memory that you need

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.470
and then decrease
the number of cores

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:14.080
that you're buying with the
VM so that you don't have

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.142
to wait to pay for
extra licensing

00:07:16.142 --> 00:07:17.350
that you don't actually need.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.810
Another great thing about
custom machine types and about

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:27.070
the general-purpose
VMs is that we're also,

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.510
aside from giving
you the ability

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.850
to create the exact shape
that you're looking for,

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:33.640
we also try to help
you with that decision

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.098
and help you with
figuring out what

00:07:35.098 --> 00:07:36.850
the right number and
the right fit is.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.930
We do this through the
rightsizing recommendations.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:46.430
So if you've been on the
Google Cloud console recently,

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.500
you might have seen a screen
that looks like this one,

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.630
and what this screen shows
you is our own recommendation

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:56.750
and the platform intelligence
that we make available to you

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.180
so that you can figure out
if what you're running today

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:03.260
is really the most efficient
way to run your workload.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:07.040
On the top here, for example,
I have an eight vCPU VM

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.960
that I'm not fully utilizing.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.780
And because of that, Google
Compute Engine right away

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:13.910
gives me a recommendation
that I can actually

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:18.770
downsize to a different VM and
directly save more than $150

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.590
per month.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.980
In many ways, this is meant
to give you the ability

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:26.510
so that with custom
machine types

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.580
and with the rightsizing
recommendation

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.770
you get the exact
fit and you avoid

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.890
overspending on infrastructure
resources that you don't need.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.539
Another thing which is
really significant and great

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:46.360
about general-purpose
VMs is that they give you

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.260
a lot of flexibility to change
the attachments that you

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.940
add to those VMs.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.070
A great example is storage.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:58.390
So on a typical day you might
think coming into the cloud

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.175
that you need locally
attached storage

00:09:00.175 --> 00:09:02.050
to be able to get the
performance that you're

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.610
looking for.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:06.430
Maybe a few days later after
some performance testing

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:09.755
you see that you actually don't
need all that performance,

00:09:09.755 --> 00:09:11.380
and you prefer to
have something that's

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:17.770
network attached and more
durable for your critical data.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:19.360
In a typical case,
you would have

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.780
had to change your
entire VM type,

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.370
choose a different one that
has local storage or not

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.660
local storage.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.120
With Google Compute
Engine, we try

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.830
to make that a
much easier process

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:33.220
so that you can just
have a VM running on GCE

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:36.360
and go and change the storage
mode or the storage type

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.170
that you are using with this VM.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:41.080
As you can see here, you
can choose the local SSD;

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.200
you can choose persistent disk,
which is network attached;

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.420
and you can even choose
among the different types

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:51.930
of persistent attached disks
that are available to you.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.080
The main goal of thinking about
our infrastructure this way

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.110
and presenting it this
way is that we simplify

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.960
the process of decision making
and we simplify the process

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.366
of operations for you.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.790
This applies to a lot of things
across Google Compute Engine.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.880
A couple of examples
have to do with how we

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:16.200
manage CPU platforms and GPUs.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.000
We simply believe that
there are decisions

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.073
you shouldn't have
to spend time on,

00:10:20.073 --> 00:10:21.990
and there are things
that an intelligent cloud

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.590
platform should be able to
do automatically for you.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:30.630
One clear example is managing
the hardware upgrade cycle.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.400
Maybe for some of
your workloads you

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.120
do care about understanding
the underlying CPU architecture

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.400
and optimizing your code for it.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.940
You might be using certain
instruction sets, for example.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.040
However, from our
experience we've

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.080
seen that most of
the time you actually

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.850
don't need to worry
about that and you

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.530
don't need to think about it.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.870
You just need something
that runs and runs well

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:53.490
on the cloud.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.680
And for these cases,
we give you the ability

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.530
to choose a minimum
CPU platform.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:01.350
As you see in the screen
under CPU Platform,

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.680
this is Intel Skylake or later.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.280
And what that means is that
by simply choosing this box

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.920
and choosing that option
you give us the ability

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:14.700
to manage the hardware
upgrade cycle on your behalf

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.410
so that we can
continue to move you

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:18.390
to the more recent
infrastructure,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.400
more efficient
infrastructure as this

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:24.060
becomes the most appropriate
one for us to use

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:27.303
and for you to use without you
having to go and do any work.

00:11:27.303 --> 00:11:28.470
You don't stop the instance.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.140
You don't recreate another one.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:31.987
It simply upgrades
on your behalf.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.910
If we're talking
about flexibility,

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:39.940
another thing that
we believe helps you

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:42.400
with general-purpose
VMs is the ability

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.860
to choose different
accelerator types.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.860
You might have been
using K80s, for example,

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.500
at a certain point of time
with now one experiment

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.200
with the new T4 GPUs.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.290
You don't need to go and rebuild
your entire infrastructure.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:59.140
Again, you can remove
one GPU and add another,

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:03.220
and you're set to go without
having to go back and figure

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:05.650
out if, for example, the
perfect shape that you've

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:08.590
been recommended also exists
with the GPU instances.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.350
So to summarize for the
general-purpose VMs,

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:21.760
we covered how a wide variety
of options and of workloads

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.310
are available with those
general-purpose VMs.

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:25.930
We talked about
how they enable you

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.240
to run things that
might be very different

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.940
and have different resource
requirements by changing

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.340
the amount of cores
and memory that you

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:37.510
have available across different
workloads from databases

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.750
to web applications, mobile
gaming, and dev and test

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:42.320
environments.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.050
We also discussed
the great flexibility

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.150
you get with
general-purpose VMs.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:48.730
You don't have to spend a lot
of time trying to research

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.650
or compare the instances.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:52.180
You get custom
machine types which

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:55.540
enable you to create an
exact fit for your workloads.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.850
And even if finding the exact
fit is not a right task,

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.990
you get recommendations
that enable you to do that.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.960
This makes general-purpose
VMs great for a wide variety

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:08.500
of workloads and makes them
really a very simple way

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.200
to get started without
having to rethink or to look

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.022
through a very large catalog
of instance-types options

00:13:14.022 --> 00:13:14.730
and compare them.

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.500
We've seen our customers make
great benefit of these features

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.690
and these capabilities
and tell us

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.850
a lot of times how custom
machine types really

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:30.680
enable them to
change their economy,

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.260
to change the economy of
running their infrastructure,

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:37.267
and spend their money and their
focus on their operations,

00:13:37.267 --> 00:13:38.225
on what really matters.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.790
Now let's talk about
bursty workloads.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:50.310
Many Google Cloud customers
look for a better option

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:54.570
to run more cost-sensitive and
bursty jobs when they need to.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.880
This can be the kind
of job where you just

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.900
need to run thousands
of cores, for example,

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:05.270
for a very short period
of time on the cloud

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:08.150
so that you complete a
certain task you have in mind.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:11.880
A good example here is
rendering jobs, for example.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.940
We see a lot of
studios coming to GCP.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.670
So they run a long-running,
a big rendering job,

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.160
get the task done in a
very short period of time

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:21.740
by leveraging thousands
of VMs at one time.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:26.860
Typically these workloads
only care about the time

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.720
it takes for them to complete.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.520
They might not essentially
care about this

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:32.332
being a real-time
process where they

00:14:32.332 --> 00:14:33.790
are interacting
with their customer

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:36.285
in every step of the way.

00:14:36.285 --> 00:14:37.660
And I'm sure that
you would agree

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:40.690
that the economics become a
very big part of this decision.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:44.020
When you're looking to run
tens of thousands of VMs

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.750
for a short period
of time, you really

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.480
don't want to be spending
too much for that.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:53.740
For these cases, preemptible VMs
is GCP's recommended solution.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:00.030
Preemptible VMs are made
for batch, checkpointed,

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:01.410
and high-throughput computing.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:09.030
They are simply short-run VMs
that can run for up to 24 hours

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.540
and can be preempted
at any point of time.

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.060
And with those
constraints, they become

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.880
80% cheaper for you to run.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:21.510
The great thing
about these VM types

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.330
is that they are actually
not a separate VM type.

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.700
It's just a flag that you
can add to any instance

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.620
at any point of time.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:30.660
You don't have to go
and redo your research

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:34.230
or think about some capabilities
you might be missing

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:38.310
if you chose preemptible
VMs, for example.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.190
This makes them ideal for
a variety of workloads,

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:46.170
from genomics to computational
chemistry to data processing

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.120
and simulations.

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:52.050
The greatest thing about them
is that you get directly 80%

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:55.350
off which is a fixed discount.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:58.080
You get predictable
pricing without having

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.650
to face any unpredictability
into this process.

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.140
As long as you have jobs
that are fault tolerant

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.120
and can manage preemption,
you directly get this benefit.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:14.160
Again, we've seen many
GCP customers make

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:16.650
great use of this capability.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.800
Many of them run tens
of thousands of cores

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.440
and tens of thousands
of VMs while saving

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.140
money, an equation,
which is typically not

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:26.000
very easy to solve.

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:38.450
Now let's move to a
different workload.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:42.440
Let's talk about
gaming as an example.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:46.460
Multiplayer games specifically
are a great example

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:49.040
of a compute-intensive workload.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:51.260
We see typically
AAA titles looking

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:53.930
for the fastest and
best experience they can

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.890
present to their end customers.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.990
For these companies,
frame-processing time

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.410
is critical.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.980
If that ended up happening
slower than expected,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.680
then their players, the
gamers, would actually

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:08.319
notice that their game is
not performing as expected,

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:10.450
and it would impact
their experience.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:17.890
This is why we've been
working for a very long time

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.040
on introducing the new
Compute-Optimized VMs, which

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.800
is one of the new instance
types that we've announced today

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:30.250
during Next 2019.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:33.310
Compute-Optimized VMs
simply provide the highest

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:37.060
per-core performance on GCP.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.000
They are the best fit for
compute-intensive workloads.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:44.200
That might include AAA gaming,
high-performance computing,

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:46.450
and a lot of very
high-performance websites

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.830
and applications.

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:51.740
These instances
provide more than 40%

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:55.890
higher performance for
workloads that are compute bound

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.800
and care about
single-threaded perf.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.350
What's great about those VMs is
that with this new introduction

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.770
they are actually powered by
the latest CPU architecture

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.250
available on the market.

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.580
So these are powered by
the new Intel Scalable

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:15.730
processors, the second
generation, codenamed Cascade

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:20.620
Lake, and they offer the highest
frequency available on GCP

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:23.980
with up to 3.8 gigahertz
in all-core-turbo.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.460
Let's talk a little bit more
about Compute-Optimized VMs.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.980
As you can see, they already
come with multiple shapes

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.160
and options.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:42.240
So today they range
from 4 to 60 vCPUs,

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:47.190
from 16 to 240 gigabytes
of memory, offering you

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:52.550
a four to one gigabyte
per vCPU ratio.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:55.880
They also enable
you to manage or use

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.740
multiple storage options.

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:01.100
So you can use network-attached
storage, persistent disk,

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:03.800
or you can use local
SSD, local storage,

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:07.740
as with any other GCE instance.

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:11.120
We're also happy that we
are introducing these VMs

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:12.720
with the new
networking caps that we

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:14.800
have available that
were announced today.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.980
So they offer directly up
to 32 gigabits per second.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:27.200
And going beyond the
shapes and the hardware,

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:30.970
we've really focused on making
the Compute-Optimized VMs

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:36.530
the best option for you when you
have near-real-time and highly

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.790
demanding workloads.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.270
So these VMs, you
get full transparency

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:43.880
into the underlying hardware.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.220
You get explicit
NUMA visibility so

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:48.260
that you get the
best performance

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:52.460
across the different VM
types and different VM sizes.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:56.950
You also get very
tight consistency

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:58.840
and very predictable
performance so

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.330
that you can bet your most
intensive applications

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.165
on those VMs.

00:20:03.165 --> 00:20:04.540
For those of you
that are looking

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:07.990
to do more fine tuning
of your performance,

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:11.850
you'll also get access to things
such as the processor power

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:15.380
state so that you can fine tune
your application performance

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:16.161
on those VMs.

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:29.470
So to summarize what we covered
for Compute-Optimized VMs,

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:31.540
these are one of our
new introductions

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:34.960
to GCP as we look to cover
more of the edge cases

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.840
that you might have for
your different workloads.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.030
They have the highest
performance per core

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.440
available on Google
Compute Engine.

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:45.580
They are powered by the
latest Intel second-generation

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:49.600
Scalable processors and
go up to 3.8 gigahertz

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:51.820
in all-core-turbo.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:55.690
In addition, they have
minimal variability, very high

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:59.020
consistency, and offer
you multiple shapes

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:01.450
so that you can find the one
thing which is looking for

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.460
and fits your workload.

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:05.870
This makes them a great fit
for multiple applications,

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:09.100
including multiplayer gaming,
high-performance computing,

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:12.220
video encoding, real-time
web serving, and others.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:22.110
Now let's move to some of
the other highly demanding

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:25.260
workloads-- different types
of highly demanding workloads.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:32.310
One great example here might
be databases, specifically

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:34.440
high-end databases.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:37.590
We see a lot of customers
running large ERP systems--

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:40.980
for example, SAP ERP systems.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:43.710
And what they are
looking for typically

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.898
is the fact that as their
database continues to grow,

00:21:46.898 --> 00:21:48.690
they are looking for
an infrastructure that

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:52.650
will grow with them and will be
able to scale with their needs.

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:54.350
While they think
about this, they

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:56.190
are also looking for
an infrastructure that

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:58.140
continues to give them
the benefits of running

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.470
on the cloud, the flexibility
that comes with that,

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.080
and the ability to have
higher fault tolerance

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:06.420
and many of the great
features that we talked about.

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:12.740
This is where the
Memory-Optimized VMs

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.520
come into the picture.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:17.570
And the way we really define
Memory-Optimized VMs on Google

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:20.490
Compute Engine is that
they are the large memory

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.910
VMs, 1 terabyte and higher.

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:26.990
These typically tend to be
multiple-socket systems, very

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:31.670
large systems, and tend to have
a very high amount of memory

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:34.190
per core so that they
can continue to scale

00:22:34.190 --> 00:22:36.800
with your memory needs.

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:41.420
Memory-Optimized VMs are built
for memory-intensive workloads,

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:46.190
including in-memory databases
and in-memory analytics.

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:48.590
They provide the highest
gigabyte per core

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:53.330
available on GCP with options
that range from 15 to 24

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:56.780
or 25 gigabytes per
vCPU, and they also

00:22:56.780 --> 00:23:00.260
offer you the lowest
dollar per gigabyte

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.090
available with these
configurations.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:07.250
Multiple shapes are
available for you

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:10.460
under the Memory-Optimized
VM category

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:15.650
ranging from 1.5 terabytes
up to the 6- and 12-terabyte

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:19.370
VMs that we just announced today
and with plans to even extend

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:20.920
up to 18 terabytes and more.

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:30.260
If you're thinking about how
the infrastructure continues

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.380
to scale with your database
and scale with your needs,

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:36.230
then one of the things
we've really had as one

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:38.540
of our guiding
principles for those VMs

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:41.240
is the speed at which we've
been trying to add new sizes

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:43.730
and continue to scale
with your requirements.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:46.910
So you can see here that in
approximately the last 18

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:51.240
months, those VMs have increased
in sizing by more than 10x

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.180
for the number of
cores they provide

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:56.555
and 60x for the amount of
memory available on those VMs.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:04.660
My favorite part about
Memory-Optimized VMs

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:06.610
though is that they
continue to give you

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.560
all the benefits of
Google Compute Engine

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:11.770
that we've just described.

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:14.290
So they are fully flexible,
and they provide you

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:18.180
with very high availability.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:20.790
Let's talk about availability.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.860
With Memory-Optimized
VMs, you continue

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:27.210
to get live migration supporting
zero downtime for your most

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:29.260
critical applications.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:31.980
And for those of you not
familiar with live migration,

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.750
it's simply our
way of making sure

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:37.740
that maintenance events are
transparent to your workloads.

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:41.760
When Google Cloud expects
or sees that a maintenance

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:45.270
event is about to
happen, we transparently

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:47.850
spin up another
version of your VM,

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.460
copy all the data
over so that you

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:52.440
don't have to
experience any downtime

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.860
or impact on your application.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:56.610
And this continues to
be one of the things

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:58.920
that we keep as a core
part of infrastructure

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:02.010
as we expand to more sizes.

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.800
Another thing here
is flexibility.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.750
If you're running the
type of deployment

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:09.210
and the type of workload where
you expect your requirements

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.270
to change months over
months, then you definitely

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:16.310
don't want to be stuck
into a reserved size

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:19.260
where moving to a different size
requires a different contract

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.100
or a different commitment.

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:23.580
And this is why the
commitments that we provide

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.040
for Memory-Optimized
VMs are really just

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.870
for Memory-Optimized VMs.

00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:33.270
You can use the same
commitment for a 1-terabyte VM.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:36.030
You can add to that and
use it for 4-terabyte VMs,

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:39.270
or you can divide it across
different VMs as you scale out

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:40.040
or scale up.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.440
It's really up to you.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:43.620
It's one big category
that you can divide it

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.440
the way you see fit as you
scale up or as you scale down.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.890
Another great thing about
Memory-Optimized VMs

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:55.540
is the simplicity.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:26:00.520
These are native GCP VMs, native
Google Compute Engine VMs.

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.700
They fully support all the
Google Compute Engine features

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:05.170
and capabilities.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:08.860
You use them directly from
the console, from G Cloud,

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:10.240
from the API.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:11.290
You can use labels.

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.793
You can use any other features
that you're using today,

00:26:13.793 --> 00:26:16.210
including the policies, for
example, that you have defined

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:19.810
for your organizations
and manage them exactly

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:23.020
as any other small VM available
as part of your deployment.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:32.130
The last part I mention about
the Memory-Optimized VMs

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:33.750
is our focus on
making sure that we

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:38.040
introduce new innovation as we
continue to expand our sizing.

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:41.310
A great example of that is the
support for the latest memory

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:44.580
technologies, and you
might have recently seen

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:47.130
some announcements from
Google Compute Engine

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.430
where we talked about the Intel
Optane DC persistent memory.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:58.910
This technology is specifically
useful for those of you

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:02.660
who have been seeing
a gap between the cost

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:06.020
and the performance available
from DRAM and that available

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:09.710
from flash or from SSD.

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:13.760
If you have applications that
require better performance than

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:18.410
storage but you cannot afford
to have them running on DRAM due

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:20.430
to the cost
associated with that,

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.060
then this fits into
that gap over there.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.430
As you can see, it
fits in that place

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:28.860
between memory and storage.

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:30.740
The other great thing
about this technology

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:33.590
is that it also gives you the
availability or the ability

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:37.695
to have persistent memory so
that for certain applications

00:27:37.695 --> 00:27:40.070
the application going down
doesn't mean that you actually

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:42.410
lose all of your data,
even though you get

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.440
the performance or a performance
that's very close to that you

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:46.030
get with DRAM.

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:54.820
Google Cloud has done a lot of
early work with this technology

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:57.740
and focused on availing it
first so that you can test it

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:00.330
on our platform.

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:03.540
With that, we give you
much higher density per VM

00:28:03.540 --> 00:28:06.030
than that which can only be
available with the regular

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:08.530
memory technologies--

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:15.080
approximately 4x more memory
at significantly lower cost.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:19.270
It tends to be much faster
and lower latency than storage

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:22.500
and is a great fit for in-memory
databases across the board.

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:29.020
Today, we do have in our
alpha testing programs

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:30.550
a few shapes available
where you can

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:34.570
test Intel DC persistent
memory for yourself

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:40.360
and add with a small node,
with a 96-vCPU node, up to 6.7

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:46.738
terabyte across DRAM and
Optane DC persistent memory.

00:28:46.738 --> 00:28:48.280
If you have workloads
where you don't

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:54.000
need the performance
of DRAM and you

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.250
want to leverage the
TCO improvements,

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:58.095
this might be a great
fit for your testing.

00:29:06.437 --> 00:29:09.020
So let's summarize the different
instance types we've covered.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:14.720
We talked about how with
Google Compute Engine

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:19.070
you get very flexible
general-purpose VMs.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.580
General-purpose VMs tend
to be the most flexible

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.440
and the most simple to use.

00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:26.780
They are great for a
wide range of workloads

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:29.820
across web serving,
databases, gaming, and others.

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:36.200
They also give you the ability
to rightsize the VM to exactly

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:37.400
what you're looking for.

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:39.320
You get recommendations
that help you

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:41.960
with making sure that you're
picking the right size.

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:43.910
They are very
flexible and enable

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:48.645
you to directly attach or
detach accelerators or storage

00:29:48.645 --> 00:29:49.145
options.

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:54.510
Then we covered the
Compute-Optimized VMs,

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:56.370
one of the new things
that we're introducing

00:29:56.370 --> 00:30:00.810
on Google Compute Engine for
your very performance-intensive

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:01.500
workloads.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:08.330
Compute-Optimized VMs offer the
highest performance per core

00:30:08.330 --> 00:30:10.610
available on GCP.

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:13.250
They are a great
fit for AAA gaming,

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:16.460
HPC, high-performance
websites, and some types

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:18.650
of scientific modeling.

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:21.050
They are available
in multiple shapes

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:25.100
and enable you to get
approximately 3.8 gigahertz

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:28.720
in all-core-turbo.

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:31.900
We talked about
Memory-Optimized VMs,

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.840
and these VMs are a great
fit for your memory-intensive

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:36.550
workloads.

00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:38.920
They have a lot of
sizes that go from 1

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:41.140
terabyte to approximately
12 terabytes

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.630
today, with more coming.

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.990
They are a great fit
for high-end databases,

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:49.600
in-memory databases, as well
as real-time and in-memory

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:50.380
analytics.

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.390
They also offer you
great flexibility

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:59.240
since you can always
scale up or scale down

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:02.790
or divide your commitments
across them in different shapes

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:03.290
and sizes.

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.820
We also talked about
preemptible VMs,

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.440
which is really a simple flag
that you can add to any VM type

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:19.510
across the different
types we talked about

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:22.900
regardless of your CPU
and memory and performance

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:24.320
requirements.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.440
And once you add that
flag, you automatically

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:30.520
get the most economic option
for bursty, fault-tolerance

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:33.190
workloads.

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:35.310
This is a great fit if
you're running a rendering

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:37.770
job, a batch job,
or some simulations

00:31:37.770 --> 00:31:41.190
that you just need compute for
but you don't care if it gets

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.860
preempted and then
continues to run again,

00:31:43.860 --> 00:31:45.570
and you have that
kind of intelligence

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:46.420
into your platform.

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:58.650
So to summarize,
today we really focus

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:01.680
on how we can make some of
your decision making simpler

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:04.680
on Google Compute Engine by
making sure that we always

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:11.460
continue to focus on simplicity,
flexibility, and efficiency.

00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:14.720
We talked about how you
can use simple guidelines

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:17.300
to make simple choices
on whether you just

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:19.970
want a wide range
of flexible options

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:22.280
with general-purpose
VMs or you're

00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:24.320
looking for the most
performant option

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:28.010
available with
Compute-Optimized VMs.

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:31.910
We also covered how, for some
of your very memory-intensive

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:34.790
applications, with
memory-optimized shapes

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:37.940
you get the benefits of
the cloud with the sizing

00:32:37.940 --> 00:32:41.210
that you're looking for, and you
get a lot of benefits related

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:44.060
to availability and
flexibility even

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:46.100
as you go to these large sizes.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:48.690
We hope that that gives
you a general overview

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:50.360
into the options
available for you

00:32:50.360 --> 00:32:52.220
and makes it a little
bit easier to decide

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:55.100
on what you're looking for
on Google Compute Engine.

00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:58.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

